WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.490
Analysis is where responders turn a possible signal into an informed assessment.

00:00:04.490 --> 00:00:08.149
The work includes triage, severity assignment, timeline building, scope review,

00:00:08.149 --> 00:00:09.978
evidence preservation, and stakeholder updates.

00:00:09.978 --> 00:00:14.369
Analysts decide what is known, what is likely, what is still unknown,

00:00:14.369 --> 00:00:16.564
and what decision is needed next.

00:00:16.564 --> 00:00:18.027
Scope is especially important.

00:00:18.027 --> 00:00:22.784
A single endpoint alert might involve one host, one user, a shared credential,

00:00:22.784 --> 00:00:26.442
a cloud service, a business workflow, or a wider pattern.

00:00:26.442 --> 00:00:31.930
Analysis should be fast enough to support action but careful enough to avoid unnecessary disruption.

00:00:31.930 --> 00:00:34.126
That means separating facts from assumptions.

00:00:34.126 --> 00:00:37.418
For example, an alert may show suspicious login activity,

00:00:37.418 --> 00:00:41.809
but the team still needs evidence about source, account behavior, session activity,

00:00:41.809 --> 00:00:43.638
affected resources, and business impact.

00:00:43.638 --> 00:00:46.931
Analysts should document confidence and uncertainty in plain language.

00:00:46.931 --> 00:00:50.590
They should also preserve evidence before actions change the environment.

00:00:50.590 --> 00:00:53.882
Good analysis helps containment become targeted rather than reactive.

00:00:53.882 --> 00:00:58.639
It gives leaders a basis for decisions, gives system owners clear next steps,

00:00:58.639 --> 00:01:03.395
and gives the response team a defensible record of why actions were taken.

00:01:03.395 --> 00:01:05.224
Good analysis is also collaborative.

00:01:05.224 --> 00:01:10.346
A SOC analyst may understand the alert, a system owner may know normal behavior,

00:01:10.346 --> 00:01:12.907
a cloud engineer may understand identity paths,

00:01:12.907 --> 00:01:15.834
and a business owner may know operational impact.

00:01:15.834 --> 00:01:20.225
Bringing those views together helps the team avoid narrow conclusions and choose

00:01:20.225 --> 00:01:22.420
actions that fit the real environment.
